// A wide creature -- the first body that is not a chain. Where an ant is a
// one-cell-wide path that flows over any terrain, this is a 2x2 block that
// translates as a unit, and the difference is entirely in `beetle.ron`'s
// `body: Rigid(...)` rather than in any code that knows what a beetle is.
//
// The trade is the point: it handles rough ground worse, and it physically
// cannot enter a one-cell tunnel. That second property is what makes an
// ant's burrow a refuge, with no "hiding" logic anywhere in the engine.
(
    name: "beetle",
    display: "Beetle",
    kind: Creature,
    density: 1.2,
    colors: [
        (86, 64, 40),
        (72, 52, 32),
        (98, 76, 50),
    ],
    flammability: 0.5,
    burn_temperature: 500.0,
    burn_duration: 60,
    burns_into: "corpse",
    heat_conductivity: 0.1,

    // **Live flesh, so a beetle is food to whatever can catch one.** It
    // authored neither of these until 2026-09-02 and defaulted to 0.0, which
    // meant `food_value` returned 0, `diet_yield` returned 0, and
    // `is_visible_prey` was false **at every gut bias** -- so nothing in this
    // world could eat a living beetle, at any point on the diet axis. That is
    // the failure class this repo keeps finding, a channel with a reader and
    // no writer: the predation mechanism is symmetric and the shipped data was
    // not. `Reports/creature-genome-flexibility-2026-09-02.md` 11a (P1).
    //
    // **200, from `beetle.ron`'s own `body_energy: 200.0`, not from the 480
    // the ant family carries.** The flesh-pricing invariant `standing_meat`
    // documents is that a material's `food_energy` equals its species'
    // `body_energy`, so that flesh bitten off a living animal is worth exactly
    // the stamp that body was granted at spawn and the ledger's live-flesh
    // seam cancels. The beetle prices its own body lower than an ant does
    // (200 x 4 cells against 480 x 2), and copying the ant's number here would
    // have made a beetle worth more eaten than it cost to build.
    //
    // `food_class: 1.0` is flesh, the same value `ant` and `corpse` carry: a
    // carnivore gut digests a beetle well and a plant gut barely at all, which
    // is `diet_yield`'s matched filter doing the work with no rule about
    // beetles anywhere.
    food_energy: 200.0,
    // In MPa like every other value of this field. **The hardest flesh in the
    // world -- a beetle's shell -- and since 2026-09-06 it is ABOVE the ant's
    // `dig_force` of 1.0 rather than under it.**
    //
    // **The constraint that held it at 0.8 is the one the graded bite
    // removed.** This comment used to read "still under the ant's dig_force
    // of 1.0, because a food priced above every shipped bite force is not
    // armoured, it is inedible" -- true while biting was a threshold, and
    // false the moment it stopped being one. Above the bite force is no
    // longer inedible; it is slower. `adjacent_food_counted` takes
    // `(bite/armour)^2` off a cell per go and banks the remainder on the
    // victim, so a mouth under the plate wears it down and several mouths
    // wear it down together.
    //
    // **1.6, and it is the setting that was measured rather than the one that
    // sounded right.** Owner: *"beetle should be stronger than an ant but it
    // can be overwhelmed or unlucky"*, and then *"it should be active"* --
    // the graded path is dormant at 0.8, because an ant's 1.0 mouth opens it
    // in one bite and nothing is ever worn. At 1.6 against that mouth:
    //
    //   damage per bite   (1.0/1.6)^2 = 0.39     three bites to take a cell
    //   a lone ant        ~12 bites for all four cells of a beetle
    //   eight ants        about one bite each
    //
    // Measured at exactly this effective armour over three seeds and 12,000
    // frames (`Reports/selective-environments-2026-09-05.md`): gnaws 385 /
    // 167 / 353 where 0.8 gives 0 / 0 / 43, ants still eating (949 / 1149 /
    // 704), and beetles roughly doubling. The balance shifts and does not
    // flip, which is the whole of what the grading bought.
    //
    // **AND IT IS BACK AT 0.8, because 1.6 INVERTS the fight it was meant to
    // settle.** Owner asked for the graded path to be active in play; set
    // there, `a_predator_eats_a_creature...` goes red and the diagnostic says
    // why: the ANT wins, taking two of the beetle's four cells and finishing
    // untouched. A lone ant is not supposed to beat a beetle.
    //
    // **BOTH HALVES ARE NOW EXPLAINED, and both are fixed -- 2026-09-06.**
    // Left here because the value below is still 0.8 and the reason it is
    // 0.8 has changed completely; the record above is what was true, this is
    // what is true now.
    //
    // The half that was understood: gnawing never swallows, so it never
    // fills the crop and never sates, and the jaw price on it was noise --
    // ~1.5 J a cell against a 200 J ant, and *constant at every armour
    // value*, because it was charged per unit of progress. A lone ant could
    // chew indefinitely. It is now charged per closure of the jaw, so armour
    // multiplies the attacker's bill instead of only its patience.
    //
    // The half that was NOT understood -- why a beetle that needs two bites
    // does not land them in 150 decisions -- was that **it could not see the
    // ant at all**. `adjacent_food_counted` scanned the eight neighbours of
    // the HEAD, and an ant eating a flank is adjacent to the beetle while
    // the beetle's head is two cells from the ant. Censused in a sealed
    // chamber over 1,200 frames: beetle-head-adjacent-to-ant **zero** times
    // against ant-head-adjacent-to-beetle **sixty**. The body now scans for
    // a living attacker on any of its cells (still a mouth for foraging),
    // and the beetle survives a lone ant to frame 162 where it fell at 101.
    //
    // **So the value below is now a balance question and no longer a blocked
    // one**, and it wants a seed sweep rather than an argument: the fight is
    // no longer inverted, but a maximally-armoured beetle still loses to one
    // ant, and the reachable range is only 0.4-1.6 (the trait multiplier
    // clamps to [0.5, 2.0] over this number). See
    // `Reports/lanes/creature-fight-handoff-2026-09-06.md`.
    //
    // The population sweep above and that fight disagreed, and it was the
    // finding: at colony scale 1.6 looked healthy, one-on-one it was
    // backwards, and the encounter was right -- a number that reads well in
    // aggregate and wrong in the thing itself is exactly what `CLAUDE.md`
    // means by looking rather than measuring. The disagreement is resolved
    // now that the encounter is not broken, and the sweep will have to be
    // re-run: every figure in it was taken on a beetle that could not see
    // what was eating it.
    //
    // Still well above the beetle's own 0.3, so a beetle cannot crack another
    // beetle in one bite -- though under grading it can now wear one down,
    // which is a change to think about before authoring a second predator.
    penetration_resistance: 0.8,
    food_class: 1.0,
)
